\begin{abstract}
Recovering three-dimensional (3D) human motion from two distant viewpoints is challenging when the subject moves rapidly and camera calibration is unavailable. We present CanonFuse3D, a calibration-free post-estimation framework that refines two approximately synchronized monocular 3D pose streams through human-centric fusion. The output consists of canonical joint coordinates relative to fixed, stream-specific body references; it does not represent a reconstructed world-space trajectory. Sequence-level pose alignment exposes cross-view agreement and disagreement, while joint features, bidirectional attention, and temporal refinement support joint-frame gated fusion with residual correction. We evaluate the framework using three skiing datasets: two for quantitative assessment and one for qualitative deployment. Compared with canonical averaging, CanonFuse3D reduces mean per-joint position error (MPJPE) on a shared body-joint subset by 44.5\% on Unity and 48.6\% on Ski-PTZ-Pose, where the latter measures agreement with a constructed canonical reference. Controlled input perturbations show gains across the tested view separations and small timing errors, with failures under severe corruption. Experiments with alternative pose estimators show that transfer depends on the upstream error distribution and benefits from target-specific adaptation. Simple learned baselines achieve lower clean-input position or acceleration errors, so the findings support the usefulness and limits of human-centric dual-view refinement rather than universal accuracy superiority.

\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
3D human pose estimation \sep dual-view fusion \sep calibration-free refinement \sep human-centric representation \sep temporal refinement

\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\linenumbers

\section{Introduction}

Recovering 3D human motion from two camera views is a computer-vision problem with practical value when a moving subject must be observed over a large area. At long capture distances, the body occupies fewer pixels, making limb localization difficult. Rapid movement introduces motion blur, changing body orientation, and short-lived occlusion. A second viewpoint can provide complementary evidence, but independently estimated monocular poses may still disagree in depth, limb configuration, and temporal continuity.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/journal/study_overview.pdf}
  \caption{System overview. Two monocular 3D pose streams are expressed in human-centric coordinates, aligned in pose space, and fused using cross-view and temporal features. CanonFuse3D produces a refined canonical pose sequence without camera parameters.}
  \label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Calibrated multi-view reconstruction addresses these ambiguities through geometric constraints. Maintaining calibration is more difficult when cameras follow a distant subject, change their orientation or zoom, or are installed temporarily in a large capture area. Existing monocular estimators also make independently predicted 3D pose streams readily available. These conditions motivate a post-estimation question: how can two such streams be combined to improve pose quality when camera parameters are unavailable?

We address calibration-free post-estimation dual-view 3D pose refinement. Calibration-free means that the refinement model receives no camera intrinsics, extrinsics, epipolar geometry, or triangulated image observations during training or inference. Its inputs are two approximately synchronized 3D pose sequences. Human-centric coordinates reduce their global position and orientation discrepancy using body landmarks, and sequence-level similarity alignment provides an aligned opposite-view pose for each stream. The resulting representation describes motion relative to fixed body references and does not recover a shared world coordinate system.

The proposed CanonFuse3D framework combines each view's pose, the aligned opposite-view pose, cross-view disagreement, and motion cues. Bidirectional cross-view attention exchanges joint information, and temporal refinement models local motion continuity. A joint-frame gate then weights view-specific pose candidates, together with a learned residual correction. This design uses complementary pose evidence after monocular estimation while keeping the upstream estimator separate from the fusion model.

Skiing provides a demanding application of distant capture and rapid human motion. We use three skiing datasets: Unity synthetic data and public Ski-PTZ-Pose data for quantitative evaluation, and author-collected indoor and outdoor recordings for qualitative deployment. Unity provides recorded avatar skeletons; Ski-PTZ-Pose is evaluated against a constructed canonical reference derived from its annotations. Controlled studies examine how the same refinement framework behaves when capture conditions or upstream pose estimates change.

The main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A human-centric framework for calibration-free post-estimation dual-view 3D pose refinement, motivated by distant capture of rapidly moving subjects.
    \item Quantitative evaluation on two skiing datasets with explicit reference regimes, complemented by a third dataset of real-world deployment videos.
    \item An assessment of refinement under viewpoint and timing changes, pose and image corruption, alternative pose estimators, and additional input views, identifying both useful conditions and failure cases.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Monocular and Sports 3D Human Pose Estimation}
Monocular 3D human pose estimation has progressed from temporal lifting models to transformer-based architectures and large-scale motion representation learning. VideoPose3D uses dilated temporal convolutions to lift 2D keypoint sequences into 3D poses~\cite{pavllo2019videopose3d}. PoseFormer models spatial joint relations and temporal correlations with transformers~\cite{zheng2021poseformer}. MHFormer represents multiple plausible pose hypotheses before synthesizing a final estimate~\cite{li2022mhformer}. MotionBERT pretrains a motion encoder on heterogeneous data for transfer to several human-centric tasks~\cite{zhu2023motionbert}. Recent full-body systems broaden the available front ends: SAM 3D Body estimates body, hand, and foot pose and shape from a single image and emphasizes generalization to diverse in-the-wild conditions~\cite{yang2026sam}, while VIBE uses a sequence-level adversarial motion prior to obtain kinematically plausible pose and shape estimates from video~\cite{kocabas2020vibe}. These methods improve the quality of an individual monocular stream, but they do not use a second synchronized stream to correct view-specific errors.

Sports footage makes the monocular problem especially difficult because professional movements are rapid, atypical of common training corpora, and frequently self-occluded. Rhodin et al. introduced a skiing dataset with rotating cameras and expert motion and used multi-view consistency mainly as supervision for a model that performs monocular inference~\cite{rhodin2018learning}. SportsCap likewise targets monocular sports videos, using semantic and temporally structured sub-motion priors to handle complex non-daily movements and severe self-occlusion~\cite{chen2021sportscap}. These studies demonstrate the value of sports-specific data and motion priors. Their inference setting nevertheless remains monocular; complementary 3D predictions from simultaneously available viewpoints are not fused after estimation.

\subsection{Multi-view 3D Pose Estimation}
A recent survey of markerless multi-view 3D human pose estimation emphasizes the benefits of complementary viewpoints while identifying occlusion, camera variation, limited supervision, temporal consistency, view selection, and the accuracy--complexity trade-off as continuing concerns~\cite{nogueira2025survey}. Classical and learned multi-view pipelines usually recover a single 3D pose inside the estimator. Learnable Triangulation combines per-view evidence through differentiable algebraic or volumetric triangulation~\cite{iskakov2019learnable}. Epipolar Transformers transfer image features along epipolar lines in a synchronized, calibrated setting~\cite{he2020epipolar}, and MVGFormer alternates appearance modules with learning-free geometric modules that triangulate refined 2D poses~\cite{liao2024multiple}. AdaFuse addresses unreliable or occluded views by estimating point correspondences between heatmaps and learning camera-specific fusion weights so that poor features do not corrupt stronger views~\cite{zhang2021adafuse}. Although these strategies use different fusion mechanisms, they operate on images, feature maps, heatmaps, or calibrated 2D observations as part of multi-view reconstruction rather than on independently produced 3D pose streams.

Other work relaxes calibration or supervision. MetaPose estimates 3D pose from multiple cameras with unknown calibration, models uncertainty in the input 2D keypoints, and requires only 2D keypoint data for training~\cite{usman2022metapose}. FLEX removes the need for extrinsic parameters by predicting view-invariant skeletal rotations and bone lengths from multiple video streams and fusing their learned features into one temporally coherent motion~\cite{gordon2022flex}. These methods substantially reduce camera requirements, but they still couple cross-view fusion to the multi-view reconstruction network. The setting studied here is complementary: the upstream estimators have already produced two monocular 3D sequences, and the refinement stage receives neither images nor 2D detections, camera parameters, or epipolar geometry.

Takahashi et al.~\cite{takahashi2018human} address the additional problem of temporal synchronization. Their method uses detected 2D human joints as correspondences and estimates camera extrinsics, inter-camera time offsets, and 3D joint positions through optimization with relaxed reprojection errors and human-body constraints. It therefore tackles unsynchronized, uncalibrated reconstruction directly. CanonFuse3D instead receives two already-estimated 3D streams and learns a pose-space correction without recovering camera parameters; it still requires approximately corresponding times. This narrower input setting does not subsume the synchronization capability of Takahashi et al. The temporal perturbation experiments and auxiliary synchronization diagnostic below characterize the remaining timing requirement, rather than establish equivalent reconstruction capability or a quantitative comparison with that method.

\subsection{Pose-Space Refinement, Reliability, and Generalization}
Post-estimation refinement provides another way to improve predictions without replacing the upstream estimator. STRIDE is a model-agnostic test-time training method that fits a motion prior to each video and refines noisy initial 3D estimates into temporally coherent poses under occlusion~\cite{lal2025stride}. Its objective is single-video temporal refinement rather than the reconciliation of synchronized viewpoints. Reliability weighting also appears within multi-view estimators: Learnable Triangulation predicts confidence weights for algebraic triangulation, and AdaFuse estimates adaptive weights for camera-view features~\cite{iskakov2019learnable,zhang2021adafuse}. In contrast, the reliability gate in this study acts after monocular 3D estimation and selects between aligned 3D pose candidates at the joint--frame level. The distinction is important because upstream confidence, image-feature quality, and residual pose-space reliability are not interchangeable signals.

Robust deployment also depends on how an estimator responds to a changed pose or camera distribution. PoseAug learns differentiable changes to posture, body size, viewpoint, and position to diversify paired 2D--3D training poses and improve cross-dataset generalization~\cite{gong2021poseaug}. AdaptPose instead generates target-like 3D motions and camera orientations from source poses and uses target-domain 2D poses without requiring target 3D labels or camera information~\cite{gholami2022adaptpose}. Both approaches adapt the training distribution of a 3D pose estimator. Our experiments address a different transfer boundary: they hold the pose-space fusion architecture fixed, replace the upstream monocular estimator, and then compare frozen transfer with target-specific adaptation of the refinement model.

These studies leave a complementary problem: fusing independently estimated 3D pose streams after monocular inference. CanonFuse3D addresses this problem through human-centric pose alignment, cross-view temporal reasoning, and joint-frame gating. The supporting experiments examine the conditions under which this refinement remains useful.

\section{Proposed Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Overview}
\label{subsec:method_overview}

CanonFuse3D takes independently estimated pose sequences \(P^L,P^R\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times J\times3}\) from two approximately synchronized views, where \(T\) is the number of frames and \(J\) the number of joints. It predicts a refined canonical pose sequence \(P^F\) of the same shape without camera parameters. Figure~\ref{fig:feature_construction} shows the five stages: human-centric canonicalization, bidirectional pose alignment, geometric-motion feature encoding, cross-view and temporal refinement, and gated pose fusion.

\subsection{Human-Centric Landmark Canonicalization}
\label{subsec:canonicalization}

Independent monocular estimates can differ in global position and orientation. We express each stream relative to a fixed reference pose \(A\) using the hip midpoint, hip direction, and neck direction. Let \(h_L,h_R,n\) denote the left hip, right hip, and neck, and let \(\operatorname{nrm}(v)=v/(\|v\|_2+\epsilon)\). The origin and body axes are
\[
\begin{aligned}
c&=\tfrac12(A_{h_L}+A_{h_R}), &
e_x&=\operatorname{nrm}(A_{h_R}-A_{h_L}),\\
e_y&=\operatorname{nrm}(A_n-c), &
e_z&=\operatorname{nrm}(e_x\times e_y).
\end{aligned}
\]
An additional landmark-direction cue resolves the sign of \(e_z\). With the axis matrix \(R=[e_x,e_y,e_z]\), the transformed coordinates are
\[
\tilde{P}_{t,j}=(P_{t,j}-c)R.
\]
The left stream, right stream, and supervision reference use separate body references. Each origin and axis matrix remains fixed over the sequences to which it is applied; the representation is therefore not a per-frame root-relative pose. Skeleton scale is not normalized, and the constructed axes need not be orthogonal. In the evaluated protocol, the reference is shared within each batch. Its exact selection and landmark conventions are specified in Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:implementation_record}.

\subsection{Pose-Level Cross-View Alignment}
\label{subsec:pose_alignment}

Because the independently anchored streams can retain residual translation, orientation, and scale mismatch, we apply pose-level cross-view alignment in both directions. Specifically, the left pose sequence is aligned to the right pose sequence, and the right pose sequence is aligned to the left pose sequence:
\[
\tilde{P}^{L \rightarrow R} = \mathcal{A}(\tilde{P}^{L}, \tilde{P}^{R}),
\qquad
\tilde{P}^{R \rightarrow L} = \mathcal{A}(\tilde{P}^{R}, \tilde{P}^{L}),
\]
where \(\mathcal{A}(\cdot)\) denotes sequence-level pose alignment.

For each sample, let \(X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times3}\) contain the source and destination joint coordinates in row-vector form, with \(N=TJ\). We estimate one uniform scale \(s\), proper rotation \(Q\), and translation \(d\) for the whole sequence:
\[
(s^\star,Q^\star,d^\star)=
\underset{s\geq0,\;Q\in SO(3),\;d\in\mathbb{R}^{3}}{\arg\min}
\frac{1}{N}\left\|sXQ^\top+\mathbf{1}d^\top-Y\right\|_F^2.
\]
We solve this unweighted least-squares problem by singular value decomposition, excluding reflections. Each direction uses one transform for all frames and joints in the sequence. These auxiliary pose-space transforms do not estimate camera extrinsics; they are also distinct from the per-frame reference alignment used only for evaluation.

The model requires dense, finite pose tensors and receives no joint-validity mask. Missing detections are represented by zero-filled raw poses, and the controlled pose-corruption study replaces or perturbs selected raw entries before canonicalization. All transformed entries participate equally in alignment and training, including corrupted or zero-filled entries; missing joints are neither interpolated nor omitted. Detection-failure flags are used only for reporting, and supervision references remain uncorrupted. Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:controlled_details} gives the intervention rules.

We further compute cross-view residuals:
\[
D^{L} = \tilde{P}^{R \rightarrow L} - \tilde{P}^{L},
\qquad
D^{R} = \tilde{P}^{L \rightarrow R} - \tilde{P}^{R}.
\]
These residuals encode disagreement between the two pose streams and provide cues about view-specific errors.

\subsection{Geometric-Motion Feature Construction}
\label{subsec:feature_construction}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/journal/method_pipeline.pdf}
  \caption{CanonFuse3D architecture. Human-centric coordinates and sequence-level alignment provide geometric-motion features for cross-view attention and temporal refinement. A joint-frame gate and residual head produce the refined pose. The feature construction in stage 4 is detailed in Figure~\ref{fig:feature_details}.}
  \label{fig:feature_construction}
\end{figure}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:feature_construction}, for each view, we construct joint-level geometric-motion features by concatenating pose, cross-view, and temporal cues. For the left-view stream, the feature is defined as
\[
X^{L}
=
\left[
\tilde{P}^{L},
\tilde{P}^{R \rightarrow L},
D^{L},
V^{L},
r^{R \rightarrow L}
\right],
\]
and for the right-view stream,
\[
X^{R}
=
\left[
\tilde{P}^{R},
\tilde{P}^{L \rightarrow R},
D^{R},
V^{R},
r^{L \rightarrow R}
\right].
\]

The velocity terms \(V^L,V^R\) describe frame-to-frame canonical pose changes, and \(r^{R \rightarrow L},r^{L \rightarrow R}\) encode the corresponding alignment rotations. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:feature_details}, the five three-dimensional cues are concatenated into a 15-dimensional feature for each joint and frame.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/journal/feature_details.pdf}
\caption{Geometric-motion feature construction for the left stream. Five three-dimensional cues are concatenated at each joint and frame: the own-view pose, aligned opposite-view pose, their difference, temporal velocity, and alignment rotation. The right stream uses the same construction with the view roles reversed.}
\label{fig:feature_details}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Joint Feature Encoding}
\label{subsec:joint_feature_encoding}

The left and right geometric-motion features are passed through a shared joint-wise encoder:
\[
H^{L} = \phi(X^{L}),
\qquad
H^{R} = \phi(X^{R}),
\]
where \(\phi(\cdot)\) is a multilayer perceptron with layer normalization and GELU activation. The encoder maps the 15-dimensional input feature into a hidden representation for each joint and frame.

Because the encoder is applied at the joint-feature level, it preserves the temporal and skeletal structure of the input sequence while producing a richer representation for subsequent cross-view interaction.

\subsection{Bidirectional Cross-View Attention}
\label{subsec:cross_view_attention}

To exchange complementary information between the two pose streams, we use bidirectional cross-view attention. For each frame, the left-view joint features attend to the right-view joint features, and the right-view joint features attend to the left-view joint features:
\[
\hat{H}^{L}
=
\mathrm{Attn}(Q=H^{L}, K=H^{R}, V=H^{R}),
\]
\[
\hat{H}^{R}
=
\mathrm{Attn}(Q=H^{R}, K=H^{L}, V=H^{L}).
\]

The attention is applied over joints within each frame. This allows the network to compare corresponding and related joint features across the two views. When a joint is unreliable in one view, the other view may provide complementary information. Residual connections and layer normalization are used to stabilize the cross-view feature update.

\subsection{Temporal Refinement}
\label{subsec:temporal_refinement}

Frame-wise fusion alone may reduce spatial error but can still produce temporally unstable pose trajectories. To address this issue, we apply a temporal refinement module after cross-view attention.

The temporal refiner uses depthwise one-dimensional convolution along the time dimension. For each joint, the hidden feature sequence is normalized and passed through a temporal convolution block:
\[
\bar{H} = \hat{H} + \mathrm{TempConv}(\mathrm{LN}(\hat{H})).
\]
This residual formulation preserves the original cross-view representation while adding local temporal context. The temporal module is lightweight and targets short-range motion consistency, which is important for reducing jitter in sports pose sequences.

\subsection{Reliability-Gated Fusion and Residual Correction}
\label{subsec:reliability_fusion}

After cross-view attention and temporal refinement, the left and right hidden features are concatenated:
\[
Z = [\bar{H}^{L}, \bar{H}^{R}].
\]
A gate head predicts a joint-frame reliability gate:
\[
\alpha = \sigma(g_{\theta}(Z)),
\]
where \(\alpha \in [0,1]^{T \times J \times 1}\). The gate controls the relative contribution of the two view-specific pose candidates for each joint and frame.

The two streams have separate body references. The network combines their view-specific candidates through a learned pose-space mapping; this combination does not imply a shared physical coordinate frame.

We construct two base pose candidates:
\[
B^{L} = \frac{1}{2}(\tilde{P}^{L} + \tilde{P}^{R \rightarrow L}),
\]
\[
B^{R} = \frac{1}{2}(\tilde{P}^{R} + \tilde{P}^{L \rightarrow R}).
\]
The reliability-gated base fusion is then computed as
\[
P^{base}
=
\alpha B^{L}
+
(1-\alpha)B^{R}.
\]

Finally, a residual head predicts a pose correction:
\[
\Delta P = h_{\theta}(Z),
\]
and the final refined pose is
\[
P^{F} = P^{base} + \Delta P.
\]

The gate \(\alpha\) is interpreted as a learned reliability signal rather than a calibrated probabilistic uncertainty estimate. This distinction is important because the model is trained to improve fusion accuracy and temporal stability, not to produce calibrated uncertainty values.

\subsection{Training Objective}
\label{subsec:training_objective}

The model is trained with a supervised pose loss and lightweight regularization terms:
\[
\mathcal{L}
=
\mathcal{L}_{sup}
+
\lambda_{view}\mathcal{L}_{view}
+
\lambda_{bal}\mathcal{L}_{bal}
+
\lambda_{ent}\mathcal{L}_{ent}
+
\lambda_{temp}\mathcal{L}_{temp}.
\]
The supervised term is \(\mathcal{L}_{sup}=\|P^F-\tilde{G}\|_1\), where \(\tilde{G}\) is the independently canonicalized supervision reference. The view reconstruction term preserves the discrepancy between the two independently canonicalized inputs. Let \(\delta=\tilde{P}^{L}-\tilde{P}^{R}\). The view-consistency objective reconstructs \(\hat{P}^{L}=P^{F}+(1-\alpha)\delta\) and \(\hat{P}^{R}=P^{F}-\alpha\delta\), and averages their \(L_1\) errors to \(\tilde{P}^{L}\) and \(\tilde{P}^{R}\), respectively. This term constrains the gate and fused estimate without treating the sequence-level similarity transforms as physical camera geometry. To avoid degenerate fusion, \(\mathcal{L}_{bal}=|\mathrm{mean}(\alpha)-0.5|\) discourages collapse to a single view, while an entropy term discourages overconfident gates during training. Finally, \(\mathcal{L}_{temp}\) penalizes excessive second-order temporal variation of \(P^{F}\), encouraging stable motion trajectories without removing the supervised accuracy objective.

The supervised and view-reconstruction \(L_1\) terms are means over all batch, frame, joint, and coordinate entries. The gate penalties average over all joint-frame weights, and the temporal penalty averages the Euclidean norm of the second difference over all joints and valid three-frame windows. No term uses an input-visibility mask to omit or reweight missing joints: an available reference still supervises the output at every joint, while a zero-filled input remains part of the view-reconstruction target. 

\section{Experimental Protocol}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

Each video view is processed independently by an upstream monocular pose estimator. CanonFuse3D and the pose-only baselines receive the resulting 3D sequences without images, 2D keypoints, or camera parameters. A separate geometric comparison group additionally receives estimated 2D joints and supplied camera calibration. All comparisons use matched sequences, joint conventions, and the stated human-centric coordinate protocol within each dataset.

\subsection{Data Sources and Annotation}
\label{subsec:dataset_annotation}

We use three skiing datasets with complementary roles: two for quantitative evaluation and one for qualitative deployment. Table~\ref{tab:dataset_summary} summarizes their inputs and reference regimes; Figure~\ref{fig:dataset_examples} shows paired-view examples.

