\label{fig:dataset_examples}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Unity synthetic dataset}
We rendered a virtual skiing scene in Unity~\cite{juliani2020} with one female and one male avatar performing 12 actions each, including steady skiing, falls, and transitions. Each action is observed from 180 synchronized viewpoints arranged in five layers, each with 36 azimuths at 10-degree intervals. An experimental sample comprises one camera pair; the model always receives two pose streams. The avatar skeleton supplies 3D ground truth independently of the monocular predictions.

\paragraph{Ski-Pose PTZ-Camera dataset}
The public Ski-Pose PTZ-Camera dataset, denoted Ski-PTZ-Pose, contains competitive alpine skiers performing giant slalom runs~\cite{rhodin2018learning,ski_poseptz_dataset}. It provides 3D poses, projected 2D annotations in approximately 20K images, and per-frame pan-tilt-zoom camera calibration. For this study, view-specific 3D annotations are aligned to a selected reference camera by similarity transforms fitted on an initial segment; the coordinate-wise median of the aligned annotations on the remaining frames defines the supervision and evaluation reference. Errors therefore measure agreement with this constructed canonical reference, rather than independent physical 3D accuracy. Neither camera calibration nor the annotation-derived transforms enter CanonFuse3D.

\paragraph{Author-collected qualitative videos}
Six indoor and outdoor dual-view skiing recordings provide a third dataset for qualitative deployment. A pose estimator processes each view independently. These videos have no independent metric 3D annotations and are excluded from training and quantitative evaluation; they illustrate the visual coherence of the refined motion relative to its input estimates and direct averaging.

\paragraph{Joint convention}
Unity uses a 15-joint skeleton, and Ski-PTZ-Pose uses a common 13-joint subset. Unity comparisons across upstream estimators additionally use this common subset, excluding the two eye landmarks. The main Unity comparison reports both subsets; the controlled native-input Unity studies use the full skeleton. These conventions apply throughout the method and evaluation.

\subsection{Implementation Details}
\label{subsec:implementation_details}

Each camera-pair sample is uniformly resampled to 30 shared frames. The refinement model uses a shared joint encoder, bidirectional attention, a depthwise temporal convolution, and linear gate and residual heads, and is trained with AdamW. Unity uses action-disjoint training, validation, and test splits; Ski-PTZ-Pose uses its predefined training and test partitions. Unity model selection uses validation error, while Ski-PTZ-Pose uses predeclared checkpoints because no independent validation partition is available. Test errors do not determine model selection. Complete architectures, optimization settings, split sizes, and checkpoint selection details are provided in Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:implementation_record}.

Pose-only comparisons include single views, canonical and aligned averaging, quality-weighted fusion, a framewise multilayer perceptron (MLP), a temporal convolutional network (TCN), and SmoothNet~\cite{zeng2022smoothnet} applied to canonical averaging. The separate geometric group includes direct linear triangulation (DLT), reprojection-filtered DLT, and DLT with a learned residual correction. Its additional 2D observations and calibration are stated explicitly in the main comparison tables.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

All errors are reported in dataset coordinate units after the stated body-coordinate transformation, without conversion to millimeters. Lower values indicate better agreement with the dataset's reference. The two reference regimes are analyzed separately.

\subsubsection{Mean per-joint position error}
Mean per-joint position error (MPJPE) averages Euclidean distances between predicted and reference joint positions:
\[
E_{\mathrm{MPJPE}}=\frac{1}{TJ}\sum_{t,j}\|P^F_{t,j}-\tilde{G}_{t,j}\|_2.
\]
Dataset-level results aggregate all evaluated joint-frame points. No additional reference alignment is applied for this metric.

\subsubsection{Procrustes-aligned position error}
Procrustes-aligned MPJPE (PA-MPJPE) evaluates pose shape after fitting a least-squares similarity transform from prediction to reference independently in each frame. The fit allows translation, a nonnegative uniform scale, and a proper rotation, with no reflection. Each reported joint subset is aligned separately. Collapsed predictions are mapped to the reference centroid and retained in the average. This reference fit is used only for PA-MPJPE and does not change model inputs, selection, or other metrics.

\subsubsection{Acceleration error}
Acceleration error measures agreement between second-order pose differences:
\[
E_{\mathrm{acc}}=\frac{1}{(T-2)J}\sum_{t=2}^{T-1}\sum_j
\|\Delta^2 P^F_{t,j}-\Delta^2\tilde{G}_{t,j}\|_2,
\]
where \(\Delta^2 P_{t,j}=P_{t+1,j}-2P_{t,j}+P_{t-1,j}\). It measures temporal agreement at the evaluated sampling interval, in coordinate units per sampled frame squared.

\subsection{Controlled Evaluation Matrix}
\label{subsec:controlled_protocol}

The following studies assess the same dual-view refinement framework on held-out Unity data. The reference model remains frozen except during the explicitly identified adaptation experiments. Full intervention and aggregation rules are given in Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:controlled_details}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Viewpoint: six 30-degree bins of circular camera-azimuth separation, testing whether complementary-view gains depend on camera placement.
    \item Timing: seven offsets spanning \(-2\) to \(+2\) frames and seven sampling-rate errors spanning \(-2\%\) to \(+2\%\), testing approximate synchronization. A separate auxiliary diagnostic estimates larger offsets up to five frames.
    \item Pose corruption: three affected-view modes, three spatial or temporal patterns, and five corruption ratios, yielding 45 conditions. Image occlusion: the same view modes and pattern names at two ratios, yielding 18 conditions before the native pose estimator. These ratios specify different interventions and do not imply equal physical severity.
    \item Upstream estimator: three alternative 3D lifting models, followed by frozen transfer, target-specific adaptation, and mixed-front-end adaptation. All alternatives lift the same ground-truth 2D tracks to isolate the lifting stage; this does not compare image detectors.
    \item Additional views: nested groups of one to four cameras, testing accuracy and computational cost when the frozen dual-view model is composed over all camera pairs. This is a pairwise extension of the refinement model.
\end{itemize}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Primary performance and cross-dataset evidence}

The expanded Unity comparison covers all 64,440 camera-pair sequences in the held-out action split. Table~\ref{tab:native_reference} reports both the full 15-joint output and the common 13-joint subset. CanonFuse3D reduces common-13 MPJPE from 0.2792 for canonical averaging to 0.1551 (44.5\%); its all-15 result is 0.1541 versus 0.2868 (46.3\%). However, the left-view baseline already achieves 0.1624 and aligned averaging achieves 0.1622, making the corresponding improvements substantially smaller. The cross-view MLP obtains lower common-13 MPJPE, 0.1361, while the TCN gives 0.1610. SmoothNet produces the lowest acceleration error in the pose-only group, 0.0113, at a higher MPJPE of 0.2925. The MLP also has lower common-13 PA-MPJPE than CanonFuse3D, 0.0561 versus 0.0637. In the additional-input group, the DLT-residual MLP reaches MPJPE 0.0964 and PA-MPJPE 0.0535; this row uses estimated 2D observations and camera calibration and is not a common-input comparison with CanonFuse3D.

The complete-split gate-error correlation is 0.650, and the gate assigns the larger weight to the lower-error view in 91.7\% of common-13 joint-frame comparisons. These diagnostics indicate that the gate carries reliability information, although its balance and entropy regularization preclude interpreting it as calibrated uncertainty or inferring that this architecture is necessary for the clean-input accuracy gain.

\input{tables/native_reference.tex}

Table~\ref{tab:ski_poseptz_reference} uses all 30 fixed Ski-PTZ-Pose test camera-pair sequences, each uniformly sampled to 30 frames on the common 13-joint representation. CanonFuse3D reduces constructed-reference MPJPE from 0.3487 for canonical averaging to 0.1794 (48.6\%), with acceleration error decreasing from 0.0392 to 0.0311. The added MLP and TCN attain lower MPJPE, 0.1423 and 0.1581, respectively. Their PA-MPJPE values, 0.1045 and 0.1144, are also lower than CanonFuse3D at 0.1269. SmoothNet achieves acceleration error 0.0151 with MPJPE 0.3227, showing that the lowest temporal error does not imply the lowest positional error. After the dataset-defined predicted-pelvis conversion, the geometric references yield MPJPE 0.3984 for DLT, 0.3996 for reprojection-gated DLT, and 0.1643 for the DLT-residual MLP. The gated variant does not improve MPJPE, PA-MPJPE, or acceleration error here. These values measure agreement with the aligned and aggregated annotation reference, not independent physical 3D accuracy.

\input{tables/ski_poseptz_reference.tex}

Across both datasets, the expanded clean-input comparison supports an improvement over canonical averaging but does not identify CanonFuse3D as the most accurate pose-only model. The framewise MLP has lower MPJPE on both datasets, while SmoothNet gives the lowest acceleration error in the direct-input groups. These results separate positional accuracy from temporal regularity and limit what can be attributed to the proposed attention and gating design. The geometric rows use additional information, and their results remain dependent on each dataset's stated reference construction.


\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Mechanism and component analysis}

\subsubsection{Reliability-gate behavior}

To examine whether the learned gate collapses to a single view or preserves local fusion flexibility, Table~\ref{tab:gate_region_summary} summarizes the predicted fusion weights on the complete held-out Unity split without input masking. The reported value is the mean left-candidate weight \(\alpha\), averaged over frames and joints within each body region; \(1-\alpha\) is the corresponding right-candidate weight. The learned gates remain close to balanced on average, which is consistent with the balance regularization in the training objective, while retaining small region-dependent variations. Together with the 0.650 gate--error correlation and 91.7\% view-preference accuracy reported above, this indicates that the gate carries local reliability information without constituting calibrated uncertainty.

