\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Robustness to capture perturbations}


The controlled studies vary one capture condition at a time while keeping the checkpoint, held-out split, and evaluation path fixed. We first test whether camera separation changes the benefit of complementary views, then measure sensitivity to short temporal and clock perturbations, and finally examine direct pose corruption and image occlusion propagated through the native pose estimator.

\subsubsection{Camera-pair separation}

The camera-angle breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:view_angle} does not reveal a failure regime. CanonFuse3D MPJPE decreases from 0.1563 in the 0--30 degree bin to 0.1528 around 120--150 degrees, while the gain over canonical averaging stays between 45.9\% and 46.6\%. Wider azimuth separation therefore supplies slightly more complementary evidence in this synthetic setup, but the narrow range cautions against claiming a strong optimum. The bins contain 8,640--12,600 mutually exclusive test camera-pair sequences and together cover the complete 64,440-sequence test split.

\input{tables/view_angle_combined.tex}

To account for the four action-specific observations associated with each camera geometry, we collapse results by unordered camera pair before inference. Every angle bin retains a positive fusion gain under paired Wilcoxon testing (all Holm-adjusted \(p<0.001\); rank-biserial correlation 0.994--1.000). The across-bin Kruskal--Wallis test is statistically significant (\(p=0.025\)) but has a negligible effect size (\(\epsilon^2=0.00049\)). Of 15 Holm-adjusted post-hoc comparisons, only 90--120 versus 150--180 degrees remains significant (\(p=0.0064\)), again with a small rank-biserial effect (\(r_{rb}=0.053\)). Thus, the fusion benefit is reliable within every tested bin, whereas camera separation explains little of its magnitude.

Detailed paired-test statistics are provided in Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:view_angle_significance}; Supplementary Fig.~\ref{S-fig:view_angle_extension} shows the corresponding absolute-error curves.



\subsubsection{Temporal offset and sampling-rate drift}

Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_sampling} summarizes two synchronization stress tests as relative MPJPE changes from each method's unperturbed value. Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:temporal_sampling} provides the absolute errors and gate diagnostics. At zero perturbation, all-15 MPJPE is 0.1541. Across \(\pm2\) frame offsets, it ranges from 0.1537 to 0.1555; the worst case, a two-frame positive offset, is only 0.91\% above zero. Gate-error correlation stays between 0.646 and 0.650 and view-preference accuracy remains above 91.4\%. The modest variation suggests that sequence-level alignment and the five-frame temporal refiner tolerate short local disagreement, but this experiment does not establish robustness to long delays or unsynchronized action phases.

Clock-rate perturbations are even smaller. At \(+2\%\), MPJPE increases by 0.23\%; negative perturbations up to \(-2\%\) change MPJPE by less than 0.02\%. Acceleration error is not monotonic because temporal resampling smooths as well as misaligns trajectories. Consequently, MPJPE is the cleaner measure of rate-drift sensitivity in this controlled test.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/extension/temporal_sampling.pdf}
\caption{Relative MPJPE change under right-stream temporal offset and sampling-rate error on the complete held-out split. Each curve is normalized by its own method-specific unperturbed MPJPE; positive values indicate increased error. Dotted horizontal lines mark zero change.}
\label{fig:temporal_sampling}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Missing-pose evidence}

Figure~\ref{fig:masking_extension} reports all 45 conditions of the complete held-out-split missing-pose study; Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:masking_extension} gives exact errors and gains at the representative ratios 0.5 and 1.0. Corrupted entries use a fixed mixture of zero replacement (35\%) and Gaussian perturbation (65\%, standard deviation 0.08 in raw input coordinate units, before canonicalization). For random masking, the ratio is the independent joint-frame probability; in the both-view condition, masks and corruption noise are sampled separately for the two streams. Distal-subset masking applies that probability only to the same eight filtered joint indices used by the executable masking protocol. Temporal masking selects the stated fraction of joints and corrupts one independently positioned contiguous 10-frame interval for each selected joint within every 30-frame sequence; its ratio therefore denotes selected joints rather than the global fraction of tensor entries.

Single-view corruption tests whether the complementary stream can rescue degraded evidence, whereas both-view corruption probes the method when redundancy is removed. The curves compare fused and canonical-average MPJPE, making explicit where learned fusion retains an advantage and where both methods fail together. At ratio 0.5, single-view temporal masking retains gains of 37.0\% for left corruption and 37.9\% for right corruption; distal masking retains 28.0\% and 31.5\%, respectively. The advantage narrows for unstructured random corruption. Most importantly, when random corruption affects both streams at ratio 0.5, fusion is 3.3\% worse than canonical averaging, whereas bilateral temporal and distal corruption still retain gains of 26.9\% and 12.5\%. Thus, the checkpoint can exploit a clean complementary stream and structured missingness, but dense independent corruption in both inputs lies outside its reliable regime.


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/extension/masking_robustness.pdf}
\caption{Complete held-out-split masking robustness for left-, right-, and both-view corruption.}
\label{fig:masking_extension}
\end{figure}

\FloatBarrier
\subsubsection{Image-level occlusion through the pose front end}

Table~\ref{tab:image_occlusion} reports all 18 conditions after the masked RGB frames pass through SAM3D and the frozen fusion model. CanonFuse3D improves on canonical averaging in 15 conditions. At random ratio 0.5, gains are 31.9\%, 34.3\%, and 20.9\% for left-, right-, and both-view occlusion, respectively. All twelve distal and temporal conditions retain positive gains, ranging from 14.6\% to 43.4\% for distal masks and from 42.3\% to 45.9\% for temporal masks. These results extend the controlled robustness evidence to errors produced by the image-to-pose front end.

Random occlusion at ratio 1.0 reverses the advantage in all three affected-view modes: fused MPJPE exceeds canonical averaging by 18.0\%, 9.2\%, and 28.4\% for left, right, and both views. In the bilateral case, MPJPE is 0.6311 versus 0.4914, and the pair-aligned SAM3D detection-failure rate is 36.36\%. Failure rates for the corresponding left- and right-view conditions are 40.51\% and 32.22\%. Failed detections remain in the evaluation as zero poses, so these results include complete upstream detection loss. The aggregate measurements do not isolate how much of the degradation comes from failed detections, errors in detected poses, or the fusion response. An unoccluded complementary view therefore does not guarantee a fusion advantage under this severe intervention.

Figure~\ref{fig:image_occlusion_extension} compares fused MPJPE for image-level and direct pose-stream interventions. Their matching ratios control different perturbations. For example, bilateral random corruption at ratio 0.5 retains a 20.9\% gain in the image-level experiment but gives a 3.3\% disadvantage in the pose-level experiment (Table~\ref{tab:image_occlusion} and Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:masking_extension}). This difference shows that the two protocols probe distinct error distributions; it does not establish an ordering of physical occlusion severity. The condition counts describe one frozen checkpoint, rather than independent training repetitions.

\input{tables/image_occlusion_summary.tex}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/extension/image_occlusion_robustness.pdf}
\caption{Fused MPJPE under image-level occlusion propagated through SAM3D and direct pose-stream corruption, for left-, right-, and both-view interventions. Solid lines with filled markers denote image-level masks; dashed lines with open markers denote pose-stream corruption. Matching protocol ratios do not imply equal physical occlusion severity. Errors use dataset coordinate units.}
\label{fig:image_occlusion_extension}
\end{figure}

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Front-end generalization and adaptation}

Frozen transfer is not uniform across upstream estimators. Table~\ref{tab:frontend_generalization} shows that MotionBERT retains a positive gain from 0.4766 to 0.4385 (8.0\%), whereas PoseFormer and VideoPose3D degrade by 18.0\% and 12.8\%, respectively, relative to their own canonical averages. The batch-referenced transform changes translation and landmark-axis coordinates but does not normalize skeleton scale or remove estimator-specific limb bias and temporal statistics. The frozen checkpoint is therefore coupled to the native front end's error distribution, and transfer to a new estimator requires validation rather than an estimator-agnostic assumption.

\input{tables/frontend_generalization.tex}

\input{sections/p1_results.tex}

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Qualitative evaluation}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{figures/journal/realworld_qualitative.pdf}
\caption{Qualitative outputs on author-collected outdoor and indoor skiing videos. Panels (a), (b), (d), and (e) show the synchronized input views; panels (c) and (f) compare the two single-view predictions, canonical average, and CanonFuse3D output. These videos have no independent metric 3D annotations and are not used for training or quantitative evaluation. These examples were also included in the earlier conference manuscript.}
\label{fig:real_world_comparison}
\end{figure}


Figure~\ref{fig:real_world_comparison} illustrates deployment on the author-collected skiing videos, where left- and right-view poses are estimated independently and no camera calibration is used during inference. The proposed result remains more coherent than either single-view prediction or direct averaging, especially when the two views disagree around arms, knees, and feet. Because no independent 3D ground truth is available, this comparison is qualitative and is not included in any reported accuracy metric. The examples also show the main limitation: if both upstream predictions contain similar errors, the refinement can still inherit part of that error.

Qualitatively, the refined skeleton tends to preserve the more stable limb configuration while avoiding abrupt compromises caused by direct averaging. This is particularly visible in poses with strong torso rotation, occluded lower limbs, or large differences between left- and right-view predictions. For sports analysis, such behavior is useful because downstream measures such as joint-angle trajectories, limb extension timing, and motion smoothness are sensitive to frame-level outliers. At the same time, the examples should be read as a post-estimation refinement result rather than as full 3D reconstruction from images; the method improves the consistency of available pose streams but does not recover information that is absent from both inputs.

\FloatBarrier
\section{Discussion}

The expanded main comparisons qualify the clean-input accuracy claim. A simple framewise MLP outperforms CanonFuse3D in MPJPE on both datasets, and SmoothNet gives lower acceleration error. The present evidence therefore supports the measured operating behavior of this refinement design, not a general claim that cross-view attention or reliability gating is required to obtain the best clean-input result. The added baselines were evaluated on the clean fixed indices. The perturbation, transfer, and view-scaling studies retain their stated comparison methods, principally canonical averaging; they do not establish robustness superiority over the newly added MLP, TCN, SmoothNet, or calibrated pipelines.
\label{sec:discussion}

The combined evidence supports a narrower claim than generic calibration-free multi-view reconstruction. CanonFuse3D is effective as a pose-space refinement layer when its two inputs are approximately synchronized, share the training front end's error distribution, and contain complementary evidence. It requires no camera parameters and retains a large advantage across the tested Unity angle bins. Pairwise composition extends the frozen dual-view model to larger camera groups, but it does not learn a joint representation over more than two views. Under the evaluated serial implementation, the number of forwards grows from one pair at two views to three and six pairs at three and four views, increasing latency from 2.02~ms to 6.02 and 12.06~ms. Because two views capture the largest accuracy gain over a single view while additional views provide smaller improvements at approximately threefold and sixfold latency, the dual-view configuration is the main accuracy--cost operating point. This timing conclusion is implementation-specific: batching or parallelizing independent pairs could reduce wall-clock latency without changing the pair count. Likewise, tolerance to small time and clock perturbations does not establish robustness to unknown long delays, dropped frames, or independently varying frame rates.

The frozen front-end comparison identifies the main deployment limitation. MotionBERT preserves a modest positive gain, whereas VideoPose3D and PoseFormer do not. This result rules out an estimator-agnostic interpretation of the current checkpoint. The adaptation matrix therefore tests heads-only, full-model, and mixed-front-end updating while retaining native-SAM3D evaluation as a forgetting check. The alternative-front-end transfer and adaptation experiments still use ground-truth Unity 2D tracks to isolate 3D lifting; comparisons that include image-detection noise for those estimators remain necessary.

The image-level study broadens the native-SAM3D evidence to include synthetic RGB occlusion and upstream detection failures. Gains in 15 of 18 conditions support a useful operating range, while the three random ratio-1.0 failures show that an unoccluded second view alone is insufficient to ensure an improvement over averaging. The masking study uses Unity projections to place artificial patches and one frozen SAM3D front end. It does not establish robustness to natural occluders, weather, motion blur, or image-level errors from alternative estimators. Those settings require separate evaluation, and the observed association between detection failures and fusion degradation does not identify a causal mechanism.

Motion blur can affect the refinement through a different error process. During an exposure, fast limb motion or camera motion spreads image evidence along a trajectory, which can reduce joint-localization reliability or cause the front end to miss the person. The resulting 3D errors may persist across adjacent frames, altering both velocity features and cross-view disagreement. A clearer second view may provide useful evidence, but similar blur-induced errors in both streams can leave the gate with little reliable information to select. Errors in the hip or neck landmarks used for the batch anchor can additionally perturb the coordinate transform. These are expected mechanisms, not measured blur effects in this study. Temporal refinement may suppress isolated jitter, but neither temporal smoothing nor the reported patch-occlusion results establish robustness to exposure-induced motion blur.

The reference regimes constrain interpretation. Unity provides an avatar skeleton in dataset coordinates that is transformed using the same batch-referenced rule; it is not a metric motion-capture measurement. Ski-PTZ-Pose is evaluated against a transformed fused reference derived from view-specific annotations; its result measures agreement with that constructed reference rather than independent physical 3D accuracy. Raw-space and transformed-space errors are therefore not interchangeable, no skeleton-scale normalization is performed, and none of these values should be relabeled as millimeters.

The body reference is shared within each evaluated batch, so changing its composition can change the representation. The results do not establish invariance to regrouping or single-sequence deployment. A sample-independent variant would require retraining and evaluation; the exact reference convention is documented in Supplementary Section~\ref{S-sec:implementation_record}.

The reliability gate is informative but not calibrated uncertainty. Its mean is explicitly shaped by balance and entropy regularization, so high gate-error correlation and preference accuracy do not establish probabilistic calibration. Because the frozen-checkpoint stress tests reuse one trained checkpoint, they do not quantify variation across independent training runs. Between-dataset uncertainty also remains unresolved. External sports datasets, metric 3D references, longer asynchronous sequences, and naturally occurring image degradation remain necessary directions.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

CanonFuse3D shows that human-centric fusion can refine two independently estimated 3D pose streams without camera parameters. Pose alignment, cross-view temporal features, and gated residual correction improve agreement with the evaluated references over canonical averaging when the inputs provide complementary evidence. The benefit is conditional: severe corruption and a changed upstream error distribution can reverse it, and simple learned baselines achieve lower clean-input position or acceleration errors. The findings support calibration-free post-estimation dual-view refinement as a useful component for difficult moving-subject capture, while emphasizing the need to validate the chosen pose estimator and capture conditions.

